## Quick start

**Once per machine:** follow the workstation set-up in the template repo (`workstation/README.md` in `{{ github_owner }}/python-project-template`). It installs mise, uv, Copier{% if has_container %}, Podman (and its machine on macOS){% endif %} and the editor extensions. Everything else (pre-commit, ruff, mypy, pytest) comes from this project's locked dev dependencies.

**Once per clone:**

```bash
mise trust                  # allow this repo's .mise.toml to run (mise refuses untrusted config)
mise install                # installs the pinned Python {{ python_version }}, uv, gitleaks{% if has_container %} and trivy{% endif %}
mise run bootstrap          # creates .venv, locks if needed, installs deps and the git hooks
cp .env.example .env.dev    # fill values locally; .env.dev is git-ignored
mise run ci                 # the same checks CI runs; must pass before you push
```
{%- if nb %}

Then register the kernel once so JupyterLab and VS Code use this repo's environment: `mise run kernel`.
{%- endif %}

**Brand-new project** (Copier just created the folder). The order matters: `git init` first, so bootstrap can install the git hooks; bootstrap before the first commit, because it creates `uv.lock`, which CI and the image build need:

```bash
git init
mise trust && mise install && mise run bootstrap
git add -A
git commit -m "chore: scaffold from python-project-template {{ template_ref }}"
```

Commit `uv.lock` and `.copier-answers.yml`. They are what make builds reproducible and updates possible.

