## Working with notebooks

```bash
mise run lab                                     # JupyterLab on this repo's environment
podman compose -f .container/compose.yaml up     # or in a container on 127.0.0.1:8888 (keep token auth on)
```

- Pick the **{{ kernel_display_name }}** kernel (register it once with `mise run kernel`).
- In the compose container your host user appears as `app` (`userns_mode: keep-id`), so saved notebooks stay yours. `.git` is mounted read-only, so code in a kernel cannot write git hooks or history: commit from the host (run `git init` before the first `compose up`). Kernels can still edit tracked files, so review `git status` before running tasks on the host after a session.
- Keep reusable logic in `src/{{ package_name }}/` with tests; notebooks import it. Start notebooks with `%load_ext autoreload` and `%autoreload 2` so edits to `src/` are picked up without a restart.
- Before sharing a notebook: *Restart Kernel and Run All Cells*, so it runs top to bottom.
- Outputs are stripped on commit (nbstripout) and notebook code is linted (nbQA + ruff).
- Keep data in `data/` (git-ignored). Never paste keys or personal data into cells: outputs are stripped, but source cells are committed.
- Optional extras for rendering and graphs: `brew install pandoc graphviz`; the `docs` extra adds the Python side (`uv sync --extra docs`).
{%- endif %}

